president trump's supreme court nominee  is back on capitol hill this morning  after a full day of answering questions  yesterday members of the senate  judiciary committee questioned  judge amy coney barrett's stance on  issues like abortion rights and gay  marriage  barrett sidestepped those topics saying  she would not forecast her decisions  she promised the senator she's made no  commitments  it would not let her personal beliefs  get in the way of the law  judges can't just wake up one day and  say i have an agenda i like guns i hate  guns i like abortion  i hate abortion and walk in like a royal  queen and  impose you know their will on the world  let me be clear i have made no  commitment to anyone  not in this senate not over at the white  house about  how i would decide any case democrats  repeatedly asked barrett if she'd vote  to overturn the affordable care act and  in one exchange vice presidential  nominee kamala harris asked her about  obamacare subscribers who have  pre-existing conditions  what wait would you give the fact that  135 million americans with pre-existing  conditions are now depending on the  protections of the affordable care act  what would you give that  i can't really say sitting here how they  would play in a way in this case because  that's part of the legal calculus of the  case  judge barrett did say that people with  pre-existing conditions would be  considered  in the case that will be argued at the  supreme court next month  though she would not specify to what  extent cbs news chief legal  correspondent jan crawford joins us from  washington now jan good morning  judge barrett is certainly not the first  supreme court nominee to sidestep  where they stand on particular key  issues what was your takeaway  from the hearings well i mean that's  right i mean she stuck to that supreme  court script that goes back  40 years to when justice sandra day  o'connor was sitting in that chair no  hints  no forecast of how they might decide any  cases or issues that might come before  them but  i thought what was notable is that she  didn't shy away  from her personal views she was very  forthcoming that she is a  woman of deep faith a catholic she's  pro-life  but she stressed that those personal  views she's never imposed them on people  in her life and she wouldn't do that as  a justice  i don't remember seeing that before that  kind of forthcoming  not dancing around the issues of how she  thinks personally  does that give us any sense of what kind  of supreme court justice she might be  jen  it does in a way but not as much as what  also i thought was a big clue from these  hearings and this was also something  that was really different she flat out  said  look i see the law i would interpret the  law a lot like my old boss justice  antonin scalia  who of course was one of the most  conservative justices on the supreme  court  she emphasized that she wouldn't be  justice scalia that she would be  justice barrett and she pointed out that  even justice scalia and justice clarence  thomas  have disagreements but justice scalia  and justice thomas  are really conservative so most people  wouldn't see a lot of daylight between  there  that was really different and in some  ways that tees up what i think could be  almost an historic  first for this nomination she could be  the first  conservative woman with that strong  overarching judicial philosophy the  supreme court has ever had she's only  had one conservative woman justice  sandra day o'connor  but she was more of a pragmatist and she  voted with liberals  quite often certainly in the back half  of her career all right jan crawford  thanks so much for being with us this  morning
 

